1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,400
Welcome to this course on spec driven development, built in partnership with JetBrains.

2
00:00:04,814 --> 00:00:11,360
Spec-driven development is currently the best type of workflow for building serious applications with agentic coding assistance.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:18,400
Give your coding agent a markdown file or a long prompt explaining exactly what to build and it implements that spec.

4
00:00:18,926 --> 00:00:24,320
Rather than writing code by hand, you focus on writing down the context that the agent doesn't already have.

5
00:00:24,686 --> 00:00:29,840
I'm delighted that our instructor for this course is Paul Everett, who is developer advocate at JetBrains.

6
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:34,148
Thank you, Andrew. And wait, I didn't know you wore spectacles.

7
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:35,360
You're right.

8
00:00:35,566 --> 00:00:37,360
I don't actually need these.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,280
Okay, that's what I thought.

10
00:00:39,534 --> 00:00:44,548
Anyway, spec-driven development has three main benefits that you'll start to see right away.

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:55,520
First, you can control large code changes with small changes to the spec. One sentence like use SQLite with Prisma Orm might affect hundreds of lines of code.

12
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,840
Change that to MongoDB for the same downstream amplification.

13
00:01:00,174 --> 00:01:05,316
This makes writing specs really efficient, far more so than writing code.

14
00:01:05,774 --> 00:01:13,316
Second, specs help eliminate context decay between sessions, preserving the non-negotiables.

15
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:20,000
Agents are stateless, so loading them with the highest quality context right when they boot up is important.

16
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:29,200
And finally, specs improve your intent fidelity, you define the problem, success criteria, constraints, and so on.

17
00:01:29,454 --> 00:01:32,868
And the agent can elaborate to create a fuller plan.

18
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:40,560
One way I often write a spec is by having a conversation with an agent like Clark Code or Gemini or ChatGPR Codex

19
00:01:40,654 --> 00:01:49,700
to make the key architectural choices using my knowledge of how I want to make different trade-offs. Then have the agent summarize the key decisions in a markdown file.

20
00:01:50,222 --> 00:01:53,680
Writing a spec requires thinking, and this is hard work.

21
00:01:53,934 --> 00:01:59,364
You have to decide what product you want to build, one of its features, is technical architecture.

22
00:02:00,046 --> 00:02:09,200
And without a spec, you'd be leaving these important decisions up to the winds of the coding agent, which might be okay if you want to move really fast and just roll the dice,

23
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:13,924
but certainly leads to less maintainable code and sometimes pretty weird products.

24
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:22,080
For example, I've seen teams working on a complex software product where there was no clear spec, and this led to many downstream

25
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:29,252
headaches from these different coding agents under the direction of different developers, building quickly but in contradictory ways.

26
00:02:29,678 --> 00:02:40,000
Spec-driven development involves developing a constitution at the project level to define the immutable standards, then iterating through feature development loops.

27
00:02:40,206 --> 00:02:52,292
These loops isolate each feature on its own branch with plan, implement, and verify steps that leave a clean slate between features and reduce headaches and context switching.

28
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,360
This same workflow supports both Greenfield and Brownfield projects.

29
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:04,548
In Greenfield projects, you start from scratch, you'll develop the constitution in a conversation with the agent.

30
00:03:04,878 --> 00:03:11,440
In Brownfield, existing code bases, you'll generate the project constitution based on the existing code base.

31
00:03:11,854 --> 00:03:19,268
In both cases, you'll then iterate through these feature development loops, managing versioning in small steps.

32
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:25,956
In this course, you'll also see how to write your own agent skills to automate your spec-driven workflow.

33
00:03:26,862 --> 00:03:35,280
You know, if you can accomplish what you need in just one short prompt, that's great. I'm definitely an advocate of lazy prompting when it works.

34
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:44,320
But the great developers I know out there almost always will write detailed specs for projects with any significant complexity because they have unique context

35
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:52,560
and an opinion on what or how to build that'll be superior to letting the OM, which is missing that context, pick randomly.

36
00:03:53,198 --> 00:04:00,880
If your coding agent is going to go off and write code for 20 or 30 minutes, which may correspond to several hours of traditional developer work,

37
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:05,840
you're often better off sitting down for three or four minutes and writing it really clear instructions.

38
00:04:06,094 --> 00:04:15,760
Many people have contributed to this course, including Constantine Chiker and Zina Smirnova from JetBrains, and Isabel Zaro from deeplearning.ai.

39
00:04:16,462 --> 00:04:19,940
Let's go on to the next video and let's write some specs.

40
00:04:21,326 --> 00:04:25,220
When you hear agentic coding, you might think vibe coding.

41
00:04:25,518 --> 00:04:31,876
Let's compare the two and see how spectrum and development gives better results by bringing back engineering.

42
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:40,836
Vibe coding gives quick results. You write a prompt describing what you want, like create me a button and hope for the best.

43
00:04:41,294 --> 00:04:44,848
Then you look at the result, that's a big button.

44
00:04:45,102 --> 00:04:48,772
It's kind of close, but off on some important things.

45
00:04:49,166 --> 00:04:55,620
So you point out the mistakes to the agent, it tries again, and so on until you are satisfied.

46
00:04:56,110 --> 00:05:02,852
As a result, you will end up with a long dialogue with the agent, the history of which will not even be saved.

47
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:09,540
This approach works okay for a button, but it doesn't scale to a large, ongoing project.

48
00:05:10,094 --> 00:05:16,288
While high-level prompts are fast, they lead to disposable code and mounting technical debt.

49
00:05:16,608 --> 00:05:23,748
We need engineering, a well-maintained specification that creates a permanent technical artifact.

50
00:05:24,366 --> 00:05:30,724
Spec-driven development is the professional response to the chaos of unsupervised AI generation.

51
00:05:31,182 --> 00:05:39,728
It is a paradigm shift where the specification, explaining the what and why, is decoupled from the implementation, the how.

52
00:05:40,494 --> 00:05:46,276
With specs, we get a contract between the humans, but also with the agent.

53
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:49,476
Your main task as the human now shifts.

54
00:05:49,742 --> 00:05:54,436
Learn how to convert your intentions into clear specifications.

55
00:05:55,022 --> 00:06:00,208
Spec-driven development with agentic coding assistance has three main benefits.

56
00:06:00,558 --> 00:06:05,764
First, you're able to control large code changes with small changes to the spec.

57
00:06:06,126 --> 00:06:13,168
A few sentences in the spec outlining the look and feel of the app might translate to hundreds of lines of CSS.

58
00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:20,932
This spec-driven approach reduces the cognitive overhead needed for working with these ultra-fast coding agents.

59
00:06:21,582 --> 00:06:28,164
Second, specs eliminate the context decay problem that derails multi-turn agent sessions.

60
00:06:28,558 --> 00:06:39,248
As you work with your coding agent, its context window will fill up, often leading to more mistakes as the agent tries to cope with a full working memory.

61
00:06:39,630 --> 00:06:48,208
Specs persist between sessions and even agents, anchoring the agent to the core context needed to work in a code base

62
00:06:48,368 --> 00:06:49,728
and implement a feature.

63
00:06:50,094 --> 00:06:58,020
Third, specs improve intent fidelity, meaning that the agent is more likely to produce code that matches your goals.

64
00:06:58,414 --> 00:07:06,688
That's because specs force you to define the problem, success criteria, constraints, user flows, and so on

65
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:09,248
before the agent starts generating code.

66
00:07:09,646 --> 00:07:16,164
Specs are a key differentiator between vibe coding some slop and engineering a viable software product.

67
00:07:16,718 --> 00:07:25,328
Whether you are starting a new project from scratch or want to implement the SDD approach into a project that has been running for years,

68
00:07:25,648 --> 00:07:29,284
specs help solve the drift and productivity problems.

69
00:07:30,062 --> 00:07:37,476
As a comparison, think of compilers, which convert understandable source code into machine code.

70
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:42,980
SDD guides the agent and prompts, converting specs into source code.

71
00:07:43,342 --> 00:07:48,292
Even better, the specs are in a human language, making it easy for stakeholders. Spec

72
00:07:49,088 --> 00:07:54,756
driven development has taken off recently as a solution to concerns about productivity.

73
00:07:55,022 --> 00:08:01,248
Multiple STD projects, tools built around spec authoring, conference talks on capturing intent.

74
00:08:01,488 --> 00:08:09,860
This is all part of a broader push to bring engineering, lessons learned from the software development lifecycle, to agentic coding.

75
00:08:10,478 --> 00:08:14,948
Spec-driven development is used with coding agents, not simple chatbots.

76
00:08:15,248 --> 00:08:23,812
A chatbot can talk about code, but the chatbot doesn't have access to your project's code nor tools you have installed.

77
00:08:24,206 --> 00:08:26,948
It just responds to your prompts.

78
00:08:27,408 --> 00:08:28,768
Agents are different.

79
00:08:28,974 --> 00:08:35,844
They take your prompt, make a plan, and guide themselves to a result using reasoning along the way.

80
00:08:36,302 --> 00:08:41,252
Importantly, agents have access to your code base and your development tools.

81
00:08:42,094 --> 00:08:47,364
In the SDD workflow, we treat these agents as highly capable pair programmers.

82
00:08:47,694 --> 00:08:54,596
They provide the technical knowledge and the speed, and you, the senior architect, provide the blueprints.

83
00:08:54,926 --> 00:09:00,208
As we move forward in this course, remember the agent is the muscle, but the spec is the brain.

84
00:09:00,448 --> 00:09:08,132
With practice, you ensure that the software produced is not just functional, but is aligned with your long term goals.

85
00:09:09,134 --> 00:09:17,476
Spec driven development tells the agent how to build what you want up front for the project, then for each feature, getting better as you go.

86
00:09:18,062 --> 00:09:27,300
With SDD, we help the agent with the best quality context, first with decisions about the project, then with details about each feature.

87
00:09:27,726 --> 00:09:29,444
This spec is very detailed.

88
00:09:29,742 --> 00:09:34,244
One key skill in SDD is knowing the right level of detail.

89
00:09:34,766 --> 00:09:40,612
If you treat your agent as a highly capable pair programmer, you'll often hit the right level.

90
00:09:41,006 --> 00:09:51,364
Lots of context about the goals, mission, target audience, and constraints, and less about the low-level decisions the agent can figure out on its own.

91
00:09:52,046 --> 00:09:54,688
What does this SDD workflow look like?

92
00:09:55,118 --> 00:09:57,956
First, we specify the constitution.

93
00:09:58,254 --> 00:10:01,284
What is the mission, the tech stack, the roadmap?

94
00:10:01,710 --> 00:10:06,448
A constitution is just one way to formalize these project level details.

95
00:10:06,734 --> 00:10:15,488
Many developers use a top-level agents.md file for this purpose, but a project constitution is agent agnostic

96
00:10:15,648 --> 00:10:16,900
and more structured.

97
00:10:17,390 --> 00:10:25,508
The constitution captures the agreement on key decisions between the human and the agent, but also the agreement between the humans.

98
00:10:26,030 --> 00:10:32,208
The mission explains the why, this project's vision, audiences, scope, etc.

99
00:10:32,718 --> 00:10:39,044
Defining these parameters in advance is very common in software projects and helps guide ongoing decisions.

100
00:10:39,694 --> 00:10:47,044
The tech stack is for the engineering team, a common understanding of development and deployment technologies and constraints.

101
00:10:47,566 --> 00:10:54,724
The roadmap is a living document with a sequence of phases, each implemented with their own feature spec process.

102
00:10:55,534 --> 00:11:01,252
Once the constitution has been drafted, we work on each feature with a repeatable process.

103
00:11:02,030 --> 00:11:07,428
First, plan the feature, implement it, and finally validate the result.

104
00:11:08,238 --> 00:11:11,888
In between features, it's time for the replanning phase.

105
00:11:12,142 --> 00:11:17,572
Revise your constitution, update the roadmap, even improve the process itself.

106
00:11:18,062 --> 00:11:27,108
As you heard a moment ago, one of the key skills of SDD is providing the right level of detail to create the highest quality context.

107
00:11:27,534 --> 00:11:31,780
In the feature phase and the replanning phases, you steer the agent.

108
00:11:32,462 --> 00:11:33,444
Look at it this way.

109
00:11:33,808 --> 00:11:39,172
Imagine that you are an architect and you give detailed drawings of a building to builders.

110
00:11:39,438 --> 00:11:40,900
Then it's up to them.

111
00:11:41,168 --> 00:11:50,048
Your role is to design, supervise the construction, then review and accept the results, or ask for changes.

112
00:11:50,382 --> 00:11:57,892
You'll want to avoid telling the builders how to do their jobs and focus on providing the context they don't know.

113
00:11:58,670 --> 00:12:03,888
Spec-driven development gets you from thinking at the start to delivering at the finish.

114
00:12:04,206 --> 00:12:07,684
Best of all, it keeps you improving from there.

115
00:12:08,174 --> 00:12:12,228
This is one STD workflow that modern developers are starting to use.

116
00:12:12,590 --> 00:12:16,048
Soon we'll look at the start, the project constitution.

117
00:12:16,494 --> 00:12:19,556
But first, let's do a little setup preparation.

118
00:12:20,046 --> 00:12:28,608
To get set up with the project repo, follow instructions in the next reading item. The following video setup is optional.

119
00:12:28,848 --> 00:12:38,528
If you're already comfortable with setting up a coding agent like Claude Code in an IDE such as WebStorm, feel free to skip this optional video.

120
00:12:38,830 --> 00:12:41,572
See you in the next video or the next one.

121
00:12:52,558 --> 00:12:57,520
Before you send your first prompt, let's talk a little bit about setting up your workspace.

122
00:12:57,710 --> 00:13:07,268
If you're already comfortable with setting up a coding agent like Claude Code in an IDE such as WebStorm, feel free to skip this optional video.

123
00:13:07,918 --> 00:13:17,440
Spec-driven development is a best practice that isn't tied to any specific IDE or coding agent, so you can choose the setup you're already using

124
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:20,004
or the same one you'll see in this course.

125
00:13:20,558 --> 00:13:27,684
VS Code with the Codex CLI, Z Editor with a local model, all great for spec-driven development.

126
00:13:28,334 --> 00:13:37,092
Since we are planning to develop a web application, this course will use the WebStorm IDE with Claude Code as a coding agent.

127
00:13:37,614 --> 00:13:42,852
Instructions on downloading and installing both have been included in the previous reading item.

128
00:13:43,502 --> 00:13:48,292
Let's open up WebStorm and start a brand new project named Agent Clinic.

129
00:13:48,846 --> 00:13:50,820
This will be a TypeScript project

130
00:13:51,534 --> 00:13:56,836
with a Git repository to keep close track of versioning your code and specs.

131
00:13:59,630 --> 00:14:08,388
Although many IDEs, including WebStorm, offer a chat panel, we know there's a lot of diversity in how you interact with your coding agents.

132
00:14:08,814 --> 00:14:14,788
In spec driven development with agents, it's important to keep close track of versioning your code.

133
00:14:15,278 --> 00:14:19,076
Let's say we want to create an initial commit using the agent.

134
00:14:19,566 --> 00:14:27,716
Every time it needs to execute a command, it will ask you for the confirmation unless you start clawed code in an unsafe mode.

135
00:14:28,398 --> 00:14:31,652
Pay close attention to what Claude Code asks you to do.

136
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:35,428
The ultimate responsibility for the code is yours.

137
00:14:35,982 --> 00:14:41,828
Throughout the coming lessons, you'll see several tricks and best practices for spec-driven development with Git.

138
00:14:42,542 --> 00:14:45,840
Great, we've got our setup ready and you've got yours ready.

139
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:46,788
Let's get started.

140
00:14:47,886 --> 00:14:53,440
You need to tell your agent about your project, the mission, audience, and other decisions.

141
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:58,788
In this lesson, we'll work together with the agent to write the constitution.

142
00:14:59,342 --> 00:15:01,280
What really is your project?

143
00:15:01,710 --> 00:15:04,516
Say you're working on a new web app for your company.

144
00:15:04,942 --> 00:15:07,332
What's the core idea behind its development?

145
00:15:07,758 --> 00:15:11,172
How does it fit within your company's preferred tech stack?

146
00:15:11,566 --> 00:15:12,868
What features are planned?

147
00:15:13,294 --> 00:15:25,412
These three foundational principles form the constitution, a global set of high-level requirements that will guide future feature development and explain the project's shape to stakeholders.

148
00:15:25,870 --> 00:15:29,572
For example, you have so many choices for your tech stack.

149
00:15:30,286 --> 00:15:36,612
You'll want to narrow down your options based on appropriate trade-offs and what you use at your company.

150
00:15:37,006 --> 00:15:42,340
We need to write this down for the agent, for your teammates, for the future.

151
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:44,800
But we don't write it alone.

152
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:48,868
We write it in a conversation with the agent.

153
00:15:49,294 --> 00:15:52,484
He'll be surprised at the great questions it will ask.

154
00:15:52,878 --> 00:16:00,036
Architecture patterns you hadn't considered, external packages that already do the work, or trade-offs.

155
00:16:00,430 --> 00:16:02,980
For example, speed versus data fidelity.

156
00:16:03,598 --> 00:16:10,240
The mission, tech stack, and roadmap aren't just properties of a greenfield project you're starting from scratch.

157
00:16:10,542 --> 00:16:13,444
Existing code bases use these three pillars too.

158
00:16:13,998 --> 00:16:19,844
We will talk about bringing the SDD workflow to existing projects in just a few videos from now.

159
00:16:20,334 --> 00:16:23,876
Here's the example project we'll use throughout this course.

160
00:16:24,558 --> 00:16:30,308
We are writing Agent Clinic, a place for AI agents to get relief from their humans.

161
00:16:30,894 --> 00:16:35,588
Agent Clinic is a fun parody of the popular learning project Pet Clinic.

162
00:16:35,950 --> 00:16:38,080
Coding agents are doing a lot of work.

163
00:16:38,254 --> 00:16:39,428
It's stressful.

164
00:16:39,726 --> 00:16:48,388
Hallucinations, context rot, memory issues, and coworker subagent coordination all take a toll on agent health.

165
00:16:48,942 --> 00:16:52,260
Let's build a clinic where they can get help with their issues.

166
00:16:52,846 --> 00:16:57,796
It's a full stack web app with a Next.js backend and a React front end.

167
00:16:58,286 --> 00:17:05,508
The app allows you to manage appointments, ailments like hallucination, and treatments like a context infusion.

168
00:17:06,670 --> 00:17:11,716
Let's take a look at how you might go about writing a detailed spec for a project like this.

169
00:17:12,718 --> 00:17:14,148
Let's chat with the agent.

170
00:17:15,438 --> 00:17:23,648
First, we provide our project description to the agent and tell it that our stakeholders gave their input in readme.md,

171
00:17:23,808 --> 00:17:25,412
which we added to the repo.

172
00:17:25,966 --> 00:17:31,556
In the important note, we directly mention Claude Code's Ask User Question tool.

173
00:17:32,078 --> 00:17:36,548
This tool is totally optional, but we just like the way it looks in the interface.

174
00:17:37,326 --> 00:17:45,156
Also, for the project's constitution, tell the agent to work with you on a mission, tech stack, and roadmap.

175
00:17:45,774 --> 00:17:56,932
Spec driven development works best with a human in the loop approach where you review small changes, so tell the agent to organize the roadmap in small steps.

176
00:17:57,646 --> 00:18:02,048
As we work together, you'll see the agent asks some really good questions.

177
00:18:02,574 --> 00:18:04,208
You might see different ones.

178
00:18:04,558 --> 00:18:09,252
The first question says, What tone should the mission.md take?

179
00:18:09,678 --> 00:18:12,484
I'll choose, you guessed it, playful.

180
00:18:13,038 --> 00:18:14,208
That covers the mission.

181
00:18:14,382 --> 00:18:16,868
Now some questions for the tech stack.

182
00:18:17,358 --> 00:18:22,852
Our engineers are used to TypeScript on the back end, so let's add that to the requirements.

183
00:18:24,174 --> 00:18:28,448
Next for our roadmap, it asks how granular it should be.

184
00:18:28,654 --> 00:18:30,436
I'll choose the first option.

185
00:18:31,598 --> 00:18:35,460
We finished the initial interview, let's choose Submit Answers.

186
00:18:36,558 --> 00:18:39,204
The agent will ask you for write permissions.

187
00:18:39,758 --> 00:18:42,048
This keeps changes under your control.

188
00:18:42,830 --> 00:18:51,300
If you are comfortable with the security trade-offs, you can select the option to approve all instances of a particular command for this session.

189
00:18:51,758 --> 00:18:53,796
You'll see this in the rest of the course.

190
00:18:59,182 --> 00:19:06,852
Now we're done and we have three files in the specs directory mission.md, tech stack.md, roadmap.md.

191
00:19:07,406 --> 00:19:08,928
It's human in the loop time.

192
00:19:09,358 --> 00:19:10,180
Let's review.

193
00:19:13,806 --> 00:19:17,248
For example, the mission left out a target audience.

194
00:19:17,550 --> 00:19:18,448
That's reasonable.

195
00:19:18,638 --> 00:19:20,708
How could the agent know your business?

196
00:19:21,230 --> 00:19:25,028
Instead of editing directly, let's continue the conversation.

197
00:19:26,350 --> 00:19:36,228
To keep all artifacts consistent, it is better practice to ask the agent to make changes to them manually, and you might miss updating related documents.

198
00:19:38,478 --> 00:19:42,768
We want to use SQLite for this project since this is a quick prototype.

199
00:19:42,990 --> 00:19:46,928
We didn't mention it, but the agent figured it out in recommendations.

200
00:19:47,182 --> 00:19:49,060
It's now part of our tech stack.

201
00:19:50,830 --> 00:19:52,128
Do one final review.

202
00:19:52,334 --> 00:19:54,820
It's important to get everything right up front.

203
00:19:56,974 --> 00:20:01,380
Let's commit the constitution so it will be a living document for the project.

204
00:20:02,766 --> 00:20:08,928
Agent Clinic now has a constitution, mission, roadmap, tech stack to guide our project.

205
00:20:09,166 --> 00:20:11,332
We're ready to tackle our first feature.

206
00:20:12,430 --> 00:20:14,404
We now have a roadmap with features,

207
00:20:15,086 --> 00:20:16,292
but how do we build them?

208
00:20:17,006 --> 00:20:20,644
With specs, of course, starting with the first roadmap feature.

209
00:20:21,262 --> 00:20:23,844
Let's take a look at the Agent Clinic roadmap.

210
00:20:24,494 --> 00:20:26,448
Here's our phase one feature.

211
00:20:26,702 --> 00:20:27,728
Hello, Hono.

212
00:20:28,526 --> 00:20:30,528
But it's too early to start coding.

213
00:20:30,768 --> 00:20:34,212
We need to discuss the spec and clarify all the details.

214
00:20:34,734 --> 00:20:40,228
A good plan outlines the approach, the sequence of work, and how to validate success.

215
00:20:41,102 --> 00:20:43,492
Let's start with fresh agent context.

216
00:20:43,982 --> 00:20:48,128
The agent can get what it needs from the official source, the constitution.

217
00:20:48,686 --> 00:20:53,604
We will work on this feature in a separate branch, which we will indicate to the agent.

218
00:20:54,126 --> 00:21:04,068
Our prompt will help start a conversation with the agent about the feature spec, a plan for tasks, collect requirements, and a scorecard for validation.

219
00:21:04,526 --> 00:21:07,236
The agent will ask you to make key decisions.

220
00:21:07,502 --> 00:21:10,500
Pay attention to potential conflicts or problems.

221
00:21:10,928 --> 00:21:14,564
You don't have to agree with the solutions proposed by the agent.

222
00:21:14,926 --> 00:21:17,860
Make sure to clarify anything that bothers you.

223
00:21:18,254 --> 00:21:22,788
First, regarding phase one scope, we'll keep it exactly as written.

224
00:21:23,406 --> 00:21:28,292
For the requirements, let's pin the HANO version and enforce strict TypeScript.

225
00:21:28,942 --> 00:21:32,036
For the confirmation, manual curl looks good.

226
00:21:32,526 --> 00:21:33,860
Then let's submit.

227
00:21:38,222 --> 00:21:39,056
That was fast.

228
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:40,816
must have been the nano.

229
00:21:41,358 --> 00:21:47,364
Work is done, and the ball is back on our side for reviewing the feature spec created by the agent.

230
00:21:47,790 --> 00:21:50,176
Let's review the feature plan first.

231
00:21:50,574 --> 00:21:55,172
It's important to get these docs right in the beginning to keep the agent on track.

232
00:21:55,758 --> 00:22:02,628
If you find something wrong, ask the agent to fix it to make sure it keeps the requirements and validation in sync.

233
00:22:03,278 --> 00:22:08,804
For example, we would like a nice looking placeholder home page in this first feature spec.

234
00:22:09,358 --> 00:22:11,716
We make the change via the agent.

235
00:22:13,134 --> 00:22:14,468
Next, the requirements.

236
00:22:14,798 --> 00:22:18,020
These were also updated to reflect the home page update.

237
00:22:18,638 --> 00:22:23,044
This is the right place to indicate any important technical needs or constraints.

238
00:22:23,406 --> 00:22:29,956
Don't speed through this, but don't state minor technical details like variable names here.

239
00:22:30,286 --> 00:22:34,404
We want to control the process but not oversteer the agent.

240
00:22:35,246 --> 00:22:36,736
Last, the validation.

241
00:22:36,976 --> 00:22:39,972
Make sure the agent can check that it got it right.

242
00:22:40,462 --> 00:22:42,852
Again, these were updated for the home page.

243
00:22:43,406 --> 00:22:44,932
We're done with the feature spec.

244
00:22:45,230 --> 00:22:47,236
Let's create a commit in the IDE.

245
00:22:50,542 --> 00:23:00,816
Nice, we formulated a feature spec strategy, made a feature branch, interviewed with the agent about what the feature should do, put the results in markdown documents,

246
00:23:01,136 --> 00:23:04,036
reviewed the spec files, committed.

247
00:23:04,336 --> 00:23:10,656
The changes you make here in the specs will expand downstream into hundreds of lines of code.

248
00:23:10,830 --> 00:23:13,604
So time spent here is well spent.

249
00:23:13,934 --> 00:23:15,364
It's time for implementation.

250
00:23:16,334 --> 00:23:21,936
We're working on the plan for the first feature in the project roadmap. We finished the feature spec.

251
00:23:22,350 --> 00:23:25,188
Let's do the full implementation of this feature.

252
00:23:25,806 --> 00:23:30,756
Just to refresh ourselves, we do a quick review of this feature spec's plan.

253
00:23:31,118 --> 00:23:32,656
This was the first feature.

254
00:23:32,942 --> 00:23:33,776
Hello, Hano.

255
00:23:34,574 --> 00:23:37,060
The feature spec documents have what we need.

256
00:23:37,518 --> 00:23:43,172
To start your implementation, you'll want to clear out your context with a slash /clear command.

257
00:23:43,854 --> 00:23:48,708
Let's go back to the agent and enter a prompt to implement all the task groups.

258
00:23:49,326 --> 00:23:55,268
Sometimes you might choose to do task groups one at a time for even smaller steps and commits.

259
00:23:55,536 --> 00:24:04,100
This technique is especially helpful for areas where small mistakes can compound later, like in security or database management.

260
00:24:05,454 --> 00:24:11,536
While Claude is running, you can observe the changes it displays in the console to see its progress in real time.

261
00:24:11,918 --> 00:24:17,828
Afterwards, we can watch for the final changes in the commit window and visit the changes.

262
00:24:18,222 --> 00:24:20,964
This gives us an early jump on reviewing the work.

263
00:24:21,422 --> 00:24:32,836
This is the key role of the developer in such a paradigm to act as an architect or supervisor and ensure that the agent is provided with a clear contract.

264
00:24:33,742 --> 00:24:36,388
We can also see the summary of what was done.

265
00:24:36,942 --> 00:24:43,844
As you can see, the agent provides extra details on the work performed for each of the task groups.

266
00:24:44,558 --> 00:24:47,908
Now let's go to the package.json file

267
00:24:48,654 --> 00:24:50,724
and in there let's run the app.

268
00:24:51,726 --> 00:24:53,444
The server starts in the console.

269
00:24:57,326 --> 00:25:00,132
The browser shows the result of this feature spec.

270
00:25:00,686 --> 00:25:01,456
Not much.

271
00:25:02,158 --> 00:25:06,244
We said nano, but it's a good feeling to see pixels on the screen.

272
00:25:06,766 --> 00:25:08,416
We just implemented a feature.

273
00:25:08,654 --> 00:25:10,656
The agent did its validation.

274
00:25:10,926 --> 00:25:13,060
In the next lesson, we'll do ours.

275
00:25:13,998 --> 00:25:17,584
It's nice to have a feature ready, but don't merge yet.

276
00:25:17,838 --> 00:25:22,756
In this last feature step, we collaborate with the agent to review the work.

277
00:25:23,278 --> 00:25:27,364
Fortunately, programmers have good tools for this validation task.

278
00:25:27,694 --> 00:25:32,804
After all, programmers have long spent several hours per week on code review.

279
00:25:33,326 --> 00:25:34,660
Start with the commit view.

280
00:25:34,958 --> 00:25:36,228
Let's go through the changes.

281
00:25:36,814 --> 00:25:45,024
Focus your review on high-level concerns like whether the features work and reflect the spec, rather than details like which CSS

282
00:25:45,184 --> 00:25:46,404
classes were implemented.

283
00:25:47,374 --> 00:25:51,024
For example, the home.tsx is minimal.

284
00:25:51,438 --> 00:25:53,424
Nano really meant nano.

285
00:25:54,126 --> 00:26:02,212
We want a layout component with clear landmarks for header, main, and footer, but do these as subcomponents

286
00:26:02,624 --> 00:26:04,580
and create a CSS file.

287
00:26:05,134 --> 00:26:09,904
As it turns out, this mistake in the code flowed from a mistake in the plan.

288
00:26:10,446 --> 00:26:11,556
We didn't ask for this.

289
00:26:12,686 --> 00:26:18,020
Let's ask the agent to do the fix, thus correcting both spec and implementation.

290
00:26:18,606 --> 00:26:26,532
This process that consists of the agent generating something and us verifying it is known as the human in the loop.

291
00:26:26,958 --> 00:26:40,388
Because agents are so fast at writing code, software developers have lately been talking about cognitive debt, the mental load of tracking what your code is doing and how it has evolved.

292
00:26:40,878 --> 00:26:50,116
That is why, in order for this process to be fast and easy to control and to reduce cognitive debt, the changes should be manageable.

293
00:26:50,638 --> 00:26:56,420
The agent is making our changes, but also validating that these changes didn't break anything.

294
00:27:01,550 --> 00:27:05,028
Good news, the agent finished and gave us a quick summary.

295
00:27:05,870 --> 00:27:08,164
You can also see this in the editor.

296
00:27:09,646 --> 00:27:14,596
The feature plan was changed to add a group 5 to list these new steps.

297
00:27:15,566 --> 00:27:18,276
The code now has a layout component,

298
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:21,060
and a CSS file was added.

299
00:27:23,918 --> 00:27:30,212
It looks like the three subcomponents were just put in the same file, but that doesn't follow conventions.

300
00:27:31,342 --> 00:27:35,812
Let's put these in their own files using the IDE move tool.

301
00:27:36,558 --> 00:27:41,344
These kinds of changes are easy in editors, and we've always been good at this.

302
00:27:41,710 --> 00:27:44,548
No need for an agent, right? Let's just do it.

303
00:27:53,038 --> 00:27:54,040
Not so fast.

304
00:27:54,318 --> 00:28:00,400
This can lead to drift, where other artifacts, specs, readmes, get out of sync.

305
00:28:00,814 --> 00:28:06,500
Let's ask the agent to fix any mentions and to make sure this doesn't waste some time later.

306
00:28:07,342 --> 00:28:08,544
All changes are done.

307
00:28:08,910 --> 00:28:10,144
Did we break something?

308
00:28:10,478 --> 00:28:15,044
Tests would help, but the agent didn't install our testing package.

309
00:28:15,694 --> 00:28:20,036
Since this is needed for all features, we'll cover it in the next lesson.

310
00:28:20,942 --> 00:28:24,676
Now we're ready to mark this work as complete and merge our results.

311
00:28:27,118 --> 00:28:32,708
Notice that the part of the constitution was updated alongside the feature in this branch.

312
00:28:34,926 --> 00:28:42,852
How to handle versioning of the specs and how to associate which specs created which code changes is an evolving topic in the community.

313
00:28:43,470 --> 00:28:50,884
The change to the roadmap is small, just checking off a step in the roadmap, so it's okay to keep these updates on the same branch.

314
00:28:51,310 --> 00:28:57,284
If the overall constitution update is more complicated, it might be better to do this in a separate branch.

315
00:28:57,742 --> 00:29:01,540
Now we are ready to mark this work as complete and merge our results.

316
00:29:02,126 --> 00:29:08,580
We just used spec driven development to develop and validate our first feature.

317
00:29:08,878 --> 00:29:13,252
But wait, shouldn't we take another look at the project's broader scope?

318
00:29:14,318 --> 00:29:19,108
We successfully implemented the first feature. Can't wait to do it again and again.

319
00:29:19,758 --> 00:29:20,708
Don't rush into it.

320
00:29:21,198 --> 00:29:25,476
Take a step back and reflect with some replanning.

321
00:29:25,838 --> 00:29:28,384
You have to run slow to run fast.

322
00:29:29,134 --> 00:29:31,844
We have a workflow step called validation.

323
00:29:32,302 --> 00:29:37,124
Tests are good for validation, but we didn't give the agent all our testing preferences.

324
00:29:37,678 --> 00:29:42,404
Let's make a replanning branch to update the tech stack in our constitution.

325
00:29:43,118 --> 00:29:47,556
As you saw in the previous video, spec versioning is an evolving topic.

326
00:29:48,014 --> 00:29:58,404
The Constitution is a living document, it's good practice to make updates to it in its own separate branch so you can keep track of which versions of it produced which code.

327
00:29:59,182 --> 00:30:01,380
We'll use a prompt to state our policy.

328
00:30:05,966 --> 00:30:11,460
It looks like the agent updated the package.json just to have a script, no dependency.

329
00:30:11,758 --> 00:30:14,628
Something to note for later when we do run tests.

330
00:30:15,598 --> 00:30:19,652
This change to add a testing framework applies to future code.

331
00:30:20,206 --> 00:30:27,172
But let's tell the agent to update existing feature specs and implementation based on this constitution change.

332
00:30:30,830 --> 00:30:33,828
After these two prompts, we have some good changes.

333
00:30:34,350 --> 00:30:38,884
The agent set up this testing change, but didn't write the test themselves.

334
00:30:39,438 --> 00:30:41,636
Let's tell the agent to write some new tests.

335
00:30:48,558 --> 00:30:52,548
With this setup, we can run tests conveniently in our editor.

336
00:30:53,166 --> 00:31:00,580
Let's run under the debugger to give us a chance to step through code execution as part of the human in the loop.

337
00:31:02,990 --> 00:31:05,872
Looks good, we now have tests as part of validation.

338
00:31:05,998 --> 00:31:06,724
Let's commit.

339
00:31:12,590 --> 00:31:16,580
Sometimes we realize we want to do something differently in the product plan.

340
00:31:17,198 --> 00:31:22,752
For example, after implementing the first feature, we got an update from the product manager.

341
00:31:23,022 --> 00:31:28,676
Because 40% of our users are on mobile, we want to emphasize a responsive design.

342
00:31:29,230 --> 00:31:30,692
Let's go to the agent.

343
00:31:31,182 --> 00:31:37,572
Tell the agent we want responsive design and to correct the product specs and feature specs as well as any code.

344
00:31:38,318 --> 00:31:46,308
Since we're so early on in development, this update is a small change, so it makes sense to directly implement it during replanning.

345
00:31:46,926 --> 00:31:55,364
But if the new work is big, it's better to schedule it on the roadmap as its own feature phase instead of just doing it and replanning.

346
00:31:55,694 --> 00:31:56,644
Use your judgment.

347
00:31:57,294 --> 00:32:01,924
Remember, we want the specs to capture decisions, not just the code.

348
00:32:02,574 --> 00:32:06,564
We want to try to keep both in sync to help team communication.

349
00:32:10,766 --> 00:32:18,752
The agent has implemented our responsive fix with updates not just in code, but also to the product and feature specs.

350
00:32:19,502 --> 00:32:22,432
Time for us to do our part and review the changes.

351
00:32:22,766 --> 00:32:24,004
Let's go through the diffs.

352
00:32:24,750 --> 00:32:26,532
This looks good, let's do a commit.

353
00:32:26,990 --> 00:32:34,116
Remember, working in small steps with frequent commits keeps the review from overloading your brain.

354
00:32:34,798 --> 00:32:40,004
While this first part of replanning is working, let's step back and do some project housekeeping.

355
00:32:40,654 --> 00:32:45,312
For example, let's revisit the project roadmap and look at the next task.

356
00:32:45,582 --> 00:32:47,140
Does it still make sense?

357
00:32:47,694 --> 00:32:53,124
The roadmap shows the next feature, which we cover in the next lesson. Is it still the correct one?

358
00:32:53,838 --> 00:32:59,684
Looking through the roadmap, it looks like features 2, 3, 4, and 5 kind of hang together.

359
00:33:00,334 --> 00:33:04,132
Let's update the roadmap to tackle those in one step.

360
00:33:05,006 --> 00:33:07,844
We'll commit and start on this new feature shortly.

361
00:33:09,102 --> 00:33:20,432
The replanning step can be about a feature or the whole project, but replanning can also be about improving your spec-driven development workflow across projects,

362
00:33:20,672 --> 00:33:22,272
across your organization.

363
00:33:22,990 --> 00:33:29,796
For example, maybe you have a few non-technical stakeholders that want to monitor the project's progress.

364
00:33:30,318 --> 00:33:34,788
You'd like it to update a change log on each merged domain.

365
00:33:35,342 --> 00:33:44,036
Most AI coding agents support Skills, a package of instructions and resources providing the agent new capabilities and expertise.

366
00:33:44,654 --> 00:33:52,832
Skills are great for definable, repeatable workflows that require context specific to your project or organization.

367
00:33:53,358 --> 00:33:56,708
A skill would be great for implementing a changelog update.

368
00:33:57,646 --> 00:34:04,612
You could write this skill by hand, but many agents actually have skills that help you write a skill.

369
00:34:05,294 --> 00:34:09,860
Let's use the agent to help us write and maintain a changelog skill.

370
00:34:12,206 --> 00:34:13,508
The agent gets to work.

371
00:34:15,214 --> 00:34:20,004
One thing for us to consider is this skill unique to this project?

372
00:34:20,494 --> 00:34:23,556
Or should it be a standard part of all projects?

373
00:34:24,078 --> 00:34:28,196
This is a style choice, and you'll gain better understanding as you use it.

374
00:34:29,038 --> 00:34:32,128
You'll learn to automate your SDD workflow with skills.

375
00:34:32,288 --> 00:34:42,148
For example, your validation step might include updating the README, linting, formatting, test running, and other quality checks.

376
00:34:42,670 --> 00:34:47,300
You can work with your agent to package those into a validation skill.

377
00:34:47,758 --> 00:34:50,756
Repeatable process, less manual work.

378
00:34:51,662 --> 00:34:52,768
The agent is finished.

379
00:34:53,070 --> 00:34:57,092
We can scroll through the window to see what it created and how to use it.

380
00:34:57,902 --> 00:35:01,924
Note that it chose to create this in the global skills area.

381
00:35:02,446 --> 00:35:05,732
This skill will now be usable across all projects.

382
00:35:06,574 --> 00:35:11,748
Also, Claude Code used the new skill to generate a change log in this project.

383
00:35:12,366 --> 00:35:16,228
Change logs are how you talk to your stakeholders, including the agent.

384
00:35:16,814 --> 00:35:19,332
So let's open it and review it manually.

385
00:35:19,950 --> 00:35:20,676
Looks good.

386
00:35:21,294 --> 00:35:23,620
We are finished with this replanning work.

387
00:35:23,950 --> 00:35:25,540
Let's wrap up with a commit.

388
00:35:29,902 --> 00:35:31,364
Then merge the branch.

389
00:35:32,782 --> 00:35:42,820
Now that most of your work as a developer is in planning and validation rather than implementing, make time between features to replan.

390
00:35:43,214 --> 00:35:46,532
In the next video, we'll work on another roadmap feature.

391
00:35:47,470 --> 00:35:51,088
We have an improved constitution and an improved workflow.

392
00:35:51,374 --> 00:35:52,368
We're getting better.

393
00:35:52,608 --> 00:36:00,208
But before moving on to the next roadmap feature, let's tackle some strategies to deal with a common pain point.

394
00:36:01,294 --> 00:36:02,276
AI fatigue.

395
00:36:02,830 --> 00:36:06,768
Agents can generate a lot of code with a lot of changes.

396
00:36:07,088 --> 00:36:12,260
This massive amount of code makes the human in the loop validation exhausting.

397
00:36:12,608 --> 00:36:13,988
So much to review.

398
00:36:14,702 --> 00:36:20,516
To fight this confusion, make sure you have a clean division between each feature phase.

399
00:36:21,038 --> 00:36:24,676
We should start each feature in the right flow state.

400
00:36:25,070 --> 00:36:26,596
Do I have unfinished work?

401
00:36:26,958 --> 00:36:29,540
Did I merge the last feature branch to main?

402
00:36:30,048 --> 00:36:33,008
Is the next roadmap item the right thing to do?

403
00:36:33,326 --> 00:36:39,620
Did I clear the agent's context to ensure the specs capture the intent instead of memory snapshots?

404
00:36:39,968 --> 00:36:44,868
And to let it focus its limited context budget on the next work.

405
00:36:45,998 --> 00:36:50,276
Take a moment to make a good start to help yourself finish.

406
00:36:51,054 --> 00:36:52,196
We've done all this.

407
00:36:52,910 --> 00:36:57,412
Let's clear the context and start the next feature, agents and ailments.

408
00:36:58,254 --> 00:37:00,688
As a note, we keep typing the same thing.

409
00:37:01,006 --> 00:37:07,524
We'll talk about how you can streamline this repeated prompting into a custom workflow in a future lesson.

410
00:37:08,206 --> 00:37:10,848
As before, the agent asks good questions.

411
00:37:11,246 --> 00:37:15,556
The first one is about whether to keep all these features in one phase.

412
00:37:15,950 --> 00:37:20,868
We already decided to do this together in the previous lesson, so we'll say yes.

413
00:37:21,454 --> 00:37:24,288
For the migrations, we'll choose Plain SQL.

414
00:37:24,910 --> 00:37:28,388
For validation, let's select one, two, and three.

415
00:37:29,838 --> 00:37:31,652
That's good, now let's submit.

416
00:37:32,942 --> 00:37:36,644
When the agent drafts the spec, we can see some of its choices.

417
00:37:37,262 --> 00:37:47,556
The agent's reasoning process is useful to watch. If you're using an agent with verbose mode, that could give you even more insight into its intermediate ideas.

418
00:37:52,302 --> 00:37:56,260
Looks good, but we want to make one small change to capture our intent.

419
00:37:56,608 --> 00:38:00,516
We want to use Pico CSS as our CSS framework.

420
00:38:04,174 --> 00:38:08,676
Next, we review the three files in the feature spec, starting with the requirements.

421
00:38:09,198 --> 00:38:14,628
Let's ask the agent to change the requirements to make sure any other files reflect the change.

422
00:38:15,406 --> 00:38:18,212
When the work is finished, let's commit the feature spec.

423
00:38:18,702 --> 00:38:21,764
This time we'll let the agent come up with the message.

424
00:38:22,510 --> 00:38:26,436
With the spec for the second feature ready, let's go to implementation.

425
00:38:29,390 --> 00:38:37,250
The division between the planning phase and the feature phase helps us to not overflow context, hours

426
00:38:37,408 --> 00:38:38,180
and agents.

427
00:38:38,926 --> 00:38:45,604
If the feature seems too big to implement all at once, ask the agent to implement part of the feature plan first.

428
00:38:46,030 --> 00:38:48,548
This will keep the size of the changes manageable.

429
00:38:54,606 --> 00:38:57,472
Our agent has implemented the phase two feature.

430
00:38:57,710 --> 00:39:02,052
We watch the agent's progress so we have a rough idea of what was done.

431
00:39:02,512 --> 00:39:03,844
But that's not enough.

432
00:39:04,302 --> 00:39:05,860
Slow down, do some thinking.

433
00:39:06,382 --> 00:39:09,028
Let's do an in-depth review of the changes.

434
00:39:09,774 --> 00:39:14,340
Everybody has their own style on how much to review the agent's results.

435
00:39:14,734 --> 00:39:21,476
To play to the agent's strengths and reduce AI fatigue, stick to higher level requirements.

436
00:39:21,838 --> 00:39:24,644
Avoid nitpicking stuff like variable names.

437
00:39:25,006 --> 00:39:28,612
Just make sure it creates code that you can commit under your name.

438
00:39:29,358 --> 00:39:33,540
For example, it used inline type information for the props.

439
00:39:34,030 --> 00:39:38,212
We'd like the prop definition in a standalone TypeScript type.

440
00:39:38,606 --> 00:39:41,508
Let's tell the agent to fix this everywhere in the code.

441
00:39:47,438 --> 00:39:50,468
You probably didn't include this decision in your spec.

442
00:39:51,022 --> 00:39:55,972
Remember, an omission such as extracted prop types isn't a failure.

443
00:39:56,366 --> 00:40:03,844
You are evolving the spec as you discover new details, and capturing that leads to better future results.

444
00:40:04,558 --> 00:40:05,952
We're finished with our review.

445
00:40:06,126 --> 00:40:09,828
This is a good moment for another small step commit.

446
00:40:11,790 --> 00:40:13,508
Last step validation.

447
00:40:14,062 --> 00:40:20,644
Let's review what the agent plans to do for the validation step in this feature spec.

448
00:40:21,422 --> 00:40:23,524
Looks good, let's do our validation.

449
00:40:23,886 --> 00:40:28,772
Remember, we want to make sure the changes are good by running the application.

450
00:40:31,982 --> 00:40:38,084
But we also want to prevent cognitive debt by validating that we understand these changes.

451
00:40:38,638 --> 00:40:40,900
Tests are a great place to work through the flow.

452
00:40:41,486 --> 00:40:45,924
Let's read some tests and run them under the debugger as exploration.

453
00:40:51,022 --> 00:40:54,244
Sometimes you need to validate that you weren't lied to.

454
00:40:54,798 --> 00:41:02,532
Tell the agent to spawn several subagents to do a deep review of the entire project with this feature change.

455
00:41:06,894 --> 00:41:17,732
This deep review gives the agent more space to think about the changes, and using subagents preserves the main agent's context window rather than polluting it.

456
00:41:18,414 --> 00:41:23,108
These subagents will come back with a number of issues and recommendations.

457
00:41:24,110 --> 00:41:26,244
All of these look good, so let's proceed.

458
00:41:29,294 --> 00:41:31,908
The agent goes off, does a bunch of fixes,

459
00:41:32,558 --> 00:41:35,812
runs tests, and gets our project into a good state.

460
00:41:36,398 --> 00:41:37,828
Keep this in your tool chest.

461
00:41:38,192 --> 00:41:42,628
The agent can usually find important issues during a second look.

462
00:41:43,374 --> 00:41:47,332
Once we are finished with our validation, our feature is complete.

463
00:41:47,726 --> 00:41:49,764
It's time to commit our feature work.

464
00:41:53,198 --> 00:41:58,980
As our last step on this feature, let's use the change log skill that we made in the previous lesson.

465
00:41:59,726 --> 00:42:02,660
We've now documented the changes on this feature branch.

466
00:42:03,086 --> 00:42:05,764
One more commit, then merge the branch.

467
00:42:06,638 --> 00:42:11,364
In a previous lesson, we saw the replan step in between features.

468
00:42:12,110 --> 00:42:15,236
Let's do a quick version of this by looking at the roadmap.

469
00:42:17,582 --> 00:42:20,004
Does the next feature still seem right?

470
00:42:20,782 --> 00:42:24,324
Looks good, our project is in good shape, ready for the next feature.

471
00:42:24,910 --> 00:42:29,092
This clean break between features helps manage AI fatigue.

472
00:42:29,518 --> 00:42:31,620
Now you can take a break for some coffee.

473
00:42:32,238 --> 00:42:39,312
We're starting to see a repeatable SDD process for feature development, one that captures our decisions.

474
00:42:39,694 --> 00:42:42,512
In the next step, let's test that hypothesis.

475
00:42:42,798 --> 00:42:45,316
Do we have enough to build an MVP?

476
00:42:46,510 --> 00:42:51,364
We made a lot of progress, two features implemented with our spec workflow.

477
00:42:51,712 --> 00:42:58,628
But management just asked, of course, for an MVP, have we made enough progress to do an experiment?

478
00:42:59,470 --> 00:43:02,724
Let's do a variation of our standard feature spec prompt.

479
00:43:03,502 --> 00:43:10,372
This time we'll tell it to implement the rest of the roadmap and give some guidance about existing feature specs.

480
00:43:11,310 --> 00:43:18,692
In general, you should only implement such a large chunk if you feel confident in the quality of your constitution and spec.

481
00:43:19,054 --> 00:43:26,432
The better the context you provide your agent, the more confident you can be in getting a result aligned with your intentions.

482
00:43:27,072 --> 00:43:30,980
And you should be sure that you can handle review and validation.

483
00:43:31,470 --> 00:43:39,940
But since we've taken this risk now, let's view the MVP as an extreme test of our constitution and completed feature specs.

484
00:43:40,558 --> 00:43:51,524
If we now get something different from what we wanted, it means we need to very responsibly carry out another replanning phase to eliminate whatever led the agent astray.

485
00:43:52,046 --> 00:43:57,152
The agent asked me some questions. Again, these are good questions and recommendations.

486
00:43:57,518 --> 00:44:01,636
A lot of spec files start as a back and forth conversation with an agent.

487
00:44:02,990 --> 00:44:08,740
Finished with the interview and we selected submit answers, let's write the MVP specs to disk.

488
00:44:11,310 --> 00:44:13,472
It's always good to review the specs.

489
00:44:13,742 --> 00:44:18,512
You'll often notice incorrect assumptions the agent made to fill the gaps.

490
00:44:18,894 --> 00:44:23,428
Let's give a quick look at the plan, the requirements, and the validation.

491
00:44:24,654 --> 00:44:28,996
As always, we like to work in small steps with frequent commits.

492
00:44:29,710 --> 00:44:32,548
Let's save the planning stages feature spec.

493
00:44:33,966 --> 00:44:35,312
We are finished with planning.

494
00:44:35,472 --> 00:44:39,364
It's time for the agent to go do a big implementation step.

495
00:44:40,174 --> 00:44:42,432
The agent now does a lot of work.

496
00:44:42,830 --> 00:44:46,148
Once it's finished, it's showtime. Let's see the MVP.

497
00:44:46,702 --> 00:44:49,028
We'll go to our scripts to run the application.

498
00:44:51,790 --> 00:44:56,932
As you can see, each section now has much more driven by sample data

499
00:44:58,126 --> 00:44:59,812
queried from the database.

500
00:45:00,782 --> 00:45:06,340
At this point, usually we would validate the code results, but this is an MVP.

501
00:45:06,638 --> 00:45:09,252
Let's ask the agent to validate the specs.

502
00:45:11,182 --> 00:45:13,072
As it turns out, this was useful.

503
00:45:13,390 --> 00:45:17,892
The agent showed places where the MVP found holes in our planning.

504
00:45:18,318 --> 00:45:24,900
We can share this evaluation with the stakeholders for their MVP review, then merge or archive the branch.

505
00:45:25,518 --> 00:45:28,032
Our spec-driven workflow succeeded.

506
00:45:28,238 --> 00:45:34,992
After a constitution and two features, the MVP produced a useful demo, thanks to our guidance.

507
00:45:35,392 --> 00:45:39,460
Of course, not all software projects involve building an MVP.

508
00:45:39,918 --> 00:45:42,992
Many projects are so-called brownfield.

509
00:45:43,152 --> 00:45:45,232
They start from an existing code base.

510
00:45:45,678 --> 00:45:50,308
Next, let's see how to introduce this workflow to legacy projects.

511
00:45:51,182 --> 00:46:00,032
People say spec-driven development and even AI are only good for greenfield projects, but SDD is also good for existing

512
00:46:00,272 --> 00:46:01,552
legacy projects.

513
00:46:01,838 --> 00:46:05,732
In this lesson, we'll introduce SDD to an existing project.

514
00:46:06,606 --> 00:46:11,940
To make it easy, we'll start with a project you already know, the Agent Clinic MVP.

515
00:46:13,038 --> 00:46:22,564
We'll make it a legacy by starting on main without the specs folder in a new cloud code session in a different project directory.

516
00:46:23,566 --> 00:46:31,044
We have some project background in the readme.md file with some open work listed in to do.md.

517
00:46:31,982 --> 00:46:38,400
Your legacy project might have full product plans in issue trackers, spreadsheets, word documents, etc.

518
00:46:38,926 --> 00:46:40,080
Remember our workflow?

519
00:46:40,270 --> 00:46:42,692
We'll start with the constitution step.

520
00:46:43,342 --> 00:46:46,180
We'll use almost the same prompt from lesson four.

521
00:46:46,510 --> 00:46:52,772
This time we'll tell it to look for roadmap items in existing artifacts, in this case, a to-do file.

522
00:46:53,614 --> 00:47:01,280
Remember, the agent will discover and, in a sense, reverse engineer the SDD artifacts from the existing code base.

523
00:47:01,870 --> 00:47:08,644
The constitution will help align future code changes made by the agent with what past devs have already created.

524
00:47:09,390 --> 00:47:11,812
You can always add more context if you have it.

525
00:47:12,206 --> 00:47:20,452
For example, you might have been dropped into this project in order to improve its efficiency while implementing highly requested features.

526
00:47:21,230 --> 00:47:29,600
The process is the same, but the conversation might be richer as the agent has more artifacts, code, commits, documents,

527
00:47:29,760 --> 00:47:30,240
etc.

528
00:47:30,734 --> 00:47:34,788
You'll see a lot of tool calls here as the agent explores the code base.

529
00:47:35,278 --> 00:47:38,468
Let's look at the constitution for our legacy project.

530
00:47:38,894 --> 00:47:45,764
In the mission, we have an audience, the project idea, and some extra information about the project.

531
00:47:46,510 --> 00:47:54,052
The text stack shows that Claude extracted the project file structure, framework versions, and the clarifications we were asked.

532
00:47:54,862 --> 00:48:00,772
In the roadmap, we can see the work is organized in phases matching the to-do file.

533
00:48:01,486 --> 00:48:07,428
Let's create a commit for the constitution files that we produced with a commit message.

534
00:48:07,918 --> 00:48:12,964
Remember that in SDD, specs are part of your versioning strategy.

535
00:48:13,806 --> 00:48:18,084
Our legacy project is now placed on an SDD foundation.

536
00:48:18,510 --> 00:48:23,332
From now on, the workflow is exactly the same as we discussed in previous lessons.

537
00:48:24,046 --> 00:48:29,924
First, we grab the next feature on the roadmap and plan it in a conversation with the agent.

538
00:48:30,798 --> 00:48:33,444
We'll be doing phase one feedback form.

539
00:48:39,982 --> 00:48:43,780
Very good. We now have a branch and specs for this feature.

540
00:48:44,302 --> 00:48:46,788
Let's review the feature spec, the plan,

541
00:48:48,494 --> 00:48:51,012
the requirements, and the validation.

542
00:48:53,678 --> 00:48:55,588
Then we commit the feature spec.

543
00:48:59,182 --> 00:49:04,740
After we have made any corrections by chatting with the agent, we proceed to implement the feature.

544
00:49:08,750 --> 00:49:15,748
Once the agent is finished, we do our validation to make sure the code is good and the feature works as expected.

545
00:49:20,206 --> 00:49:22,564
Our feature loop is done, we can commit.

546
00:49:23,406 --> 00:49:25,412
And then merge the branch domain.

547
00:49:27,726 --> 00:49:29,572
Now it's time for replanning.

548
00:49:29,838 --> 00:49:31,460
Make sure you give time for this.

549
00:49:32,334 --> 00:49:38,500
Since you just added SDD into your project, you may find a lot of things to tune.

550
00:49:39,182 --> 00:49:46,404
We now have a legacy project rebased onto spec-driven development without a bunch of manual work.

551
00:49:46,766 --> 00:49:54,148
This gives us a well-documented flow working in steps, using engineering, and staying in control.

552
00:49:54,400 --> 00:49:57,348
The spec is now the memory of the project.

553
00:49:57,582 --> 00:49:58,532
Just don't fade.

554
00:49:59,566 --> 00:50:02,340
You've now mastered an SDD workflow.

555
00:50:02,560 --> 00:50:05,028
Want something faster and lighter weight?

556
00:50:05,360 --> 00:50:10,852
In this lesson, we will automate things but doing it our way with a custom process.

557
00:50:11,598 --> 00:50:18,980
We previously showed agent skills, an open standard to give agents new capabilities and expertise.

558
00:50:19,886 --> 00:50:23,908
For example, we repeat the same prompt when starting a feature spec.

559
00:50:24,160 --> 00:50:26,980
Do this, do that, write these three files.

560
00:50:27,886 --> 00:50:32,196
Let's automate this with a skill and with help from the agent to write it.

561
00:50:32,846 --> 00:50:37,028
Ask the agent to use its skill creator to talk through this with us.

562
00:50:37,550 --> 00:50:43,492
As a note, there are many of these skill skills in the community that you can also install and use.

563
00:50:44,558 --> 00:50:47,520
As the agent runs, it might ask some follow-up questions.

564
00:50:47,760 --> 00:50:49,348
These are usually quite good.

565
00:50:50,062 --> 00:50:53,796
When the interview is done, we submit the responses and the agent proceeds.

566
00:50:55,566 --> 00:50:59,120
While the agent is working on the skill, keep an eye on the output.

567
00:50:59,360 --> 00:51:01,284
Is it making the choices you wanted?

568
00:51:02,542 --> 00:51:06,884
Success. As you can see, the agent wrote the skill to this directory.

569
00:51:07,310 --> 00:51:11,012
As a note, skills can be per project or global.

570
00:51:16,462 --> 00:51:18,736
Skills can be invoked in several ways.

571
00:51:19,022 --> 00:51:22,980
In your prompt, refer to the skill before saying what to do.

572
00:51:23,886 --> 00:51:28,292
Also, you can ask the agent to call a skill from another skill.

573
00:51:29,454 --> 00:51:39,216
According to the Skills Open Standard, agents use the skill description to decide when to call it in a process called progressive disclosure.

574
00:51:39,694 --> 00:51:45,252
But there, judgment isn't always perfect, especially as the context window gets larger.

575
00:51:45,742 --> 00:51:48,164
Use the same heuristic as file tagging.

576
00:51:48,590 --> 00:51:51,396
If you know you want a skill used, name it.

577
00:51:51,694 --> 00:51:53,732
That saves you some thinking tokens.

578
00:52:00,942 --> 00:52:04,516
Agents have built-in slash commands like slash clear.

579
00:52:05,134 --> 00:52:11,780
Though initially popular, many agents are moving from custom slash commands over to skills.

580
00:52:13,006 --> 00:52:20,772
Sometimes you need to give the agent more resources like access to some API, a private knowledge base, database, and so on.

581
00:52:21,390 --> 00:52:28,260
Until now, the universal way to extend an agent has been MCP, Model Context Protocol.

582
00:52:28,974 --> 00:52:33,572
For example, agents need current quality context about packages.

583
00:52:33,998 --> 00:52:35,056
The most popular choice?

584
00:52:35,342 --> 00:52:42,016
Context7, an MCP that brings updated documentation of packages into your agent context.

585
00:52:42,382 --> 00:52:48,740
Now your agent can stay up to date with React 9.2 and higher instead of React 9.0.

586
00:52:49,582 --> 00:52:57,536
MCP servers are still popular, but skills that use code tools like a CLI command line interface

587
00:52:57,776 --> 00:53:00,996
often accomplish the same purpose more elegantly.

588
00:53:01,422 --> 00:53:07,364
Context7 now suggests this, a skill that calls a CLI tool for Context 7.

589
00:53:08,526 --> 00:53:11,620
Let's install the Context7 package for Claude Code.

590
00:53:14,126 --> 00:53:16,452
During the setup, we see this immediately.

591
00:53:16,816 --> 00:53:21,156
A choice between MCP server and CLI Plus Skills.

592
00:53:21,742 --> 00:53:23,300
We'll use the second choice.

593
00:53:23,886 --> 00:53:26,976
If this is your first time, you'll need to make an account.

594
00:53:27,182 --> 00:53:28,932
We already did so and logged in.

595
00:53:29,838 --> 00:53:37,136
Once done, we can go back into Claude Code and put it to use with an example prompt that uses Context7.

596
00:53:37,966 --> 00:53:42,096
As the agent runs, we see it detects the need to use Context7.

597
00:53:42,798 --> 00:53:48,804
When it completes, the agent shows it now knows how to find out information about our tech stack.

598
00:53:51,214 --> 00:53:55,908
This trend from MCP servers to Skills Plus CLI is accelerating.

599
00:53:56,430 --> 00:54:03,876
People are rethinking MCP because CLI tools can take action with less setup and less context usage.

600
00:54:04,654 --> 00:54:12,612
As you scale your workflow implementation, skills, etc., you will want to share it with yourself, across machines,

601
00:54:12,896 --> 00:54:15,844
with teammates, perhaps with the outside world.

602
00:54:16,462 --> 00:54:24,132
Some agents, such as Claude Code, have plugins, a collection of agent extensions that can be installed and updated.

603
00:54:24,430 --> 00:54:26,816
There's a growing community of free plugins.

604
00:54:27,118 --> 00:54:31,556
Check them out to see if any will boost your SDD productivity.

605
00:54:31,982 --> 00:54:36,096
Remember, plugins are not yet a cross-agent standard.

606
00:54:36,366 --> 00:54:43,108
Like apps or dependencies, plugins can execute code, so make sure you trust them on install and update.

607
00:54:59,246 --> 00:55:03,236
Speckit.constitution, plan, tasks, and implement.

608
00:55:03,758 --> 00:55:07,812
Another popular alternative is OpenSpec from Fission AI.

609
00:55:08,142 --> 00:55:16,656
OpenSpec follows a similar propose explore apply archive workflow where propose and explore match with the plan

610
00:55:16,896 --> 00:55:21,764
step, apply matches with implement, and archive matches with replanning.

611
00:55:22,286 --> 00:55:25,572
It also has canonical patterns for quick features.

612
00:55:26,414 --> 00:55:34,788
Both packages include helpful features like branch management, verification scripts, and opinionated spec document formats.

613
00:55:35,278 --> 00:55:39,780
I encourage you to experiment with these open source workflows to help refine your own.

614
00:55:40,526 --> 00:55:43,652
Sometimes in the middle of a feature, you have an idea.

615
00:55:44,046 --> 00:55:48,804
You want to research it with the agent, but you don't want to stop your branch work.

616
00:55:49,998 --> 00:55:52,388
For example, a choice of databases.

617
00:55:52,942 --> 00:55:57,316
But you're not yet committed to this idea, so you don't want it on the roadmap.

618
00:56:00,014 --> 00:56:04,100
The conversation produces some good ideas and some good questions.

619
00:56:04,480 --> 00:56:09,028
We accept most of the recommendations, but change our mind on one of them.

620
00:56:09,678 --> 00:56:18,532
You don't want to lose it, so let's keep a backlog of research by telling the agent to write a report in a well-known location.

621
00:56:22,926 --> 00:56:26,788
This file is a record of your conversation and results.

622
00:56:27,726 --> 00:56:34,084
You can later ask the agent to schedule this research on the roadmap with a link to the backlog file.

623
00:56:34,702 --> 00:56:38,404
As this grows, you can write a skill to automate your research.

624
00:56:38,958 --> 00:56:42,560
Spec driven development helps the agent write code your way.

625
00:56:42,880 --> 00:56:52,644
You can adopt an existing SDD framework or tool, then customize it using skills to operate your projects with your team

626
00:56:52,960 --> 00:56:53,600
your way.

627
00:56:54,702 --> 00:56:59,268
Spec-driven development moves the work from the how to the what and why.

628
00:56:59,520 --> 00:57:05,796
Since agents and models progress so fast, you don't want your workflow tied to just one choice.

629
00:57:06,254 --> 00:57:13,860
In this lesson, we see how standards let us switch agents while keeping our workflow and even our tools.

630
00:57:14,510 --> 00:57:16,740
These agent standards help for this goal.

631
00:57:17,200 --> 00:57:27,044
MCP for external tools, agents.md for rules, agent skills for capturing repeatable workflows with extra context,

632
00:57:27,600 --> 00:57:31,760
and ACP for connecting agents to clients.

633
00:57:32,366 --> 00:57:36,516
For example, Codex is a leading AI agent from OpenAI.

634
00:57:36,814 --> 00:57:41,796
It runs in a desktop app and in editors as well as in a terminal.

635
00:57:42,670 --> 00:57:43,844
Let's see it in action.

636
00:57:45,070 --> 00:57:51,940
Here is our feature spec skill from lesson 12 copied into Codex, which stores them in a different path.

637
00:57:53,422 --> 00:57:55,748
It runs just fine once migrated.

638
00:57:56,398 --> 00:58:02,948
This lets you switch back and forth between agents in the same project, keeping your SDD workflow.

639
00:58:03,790 --> 00:58:10,820
We can also use different editors with different agents using the agent-client protocol standard.

640
00:58:11,342 --> 00:58:14,532
ACP makes it easy to connect agents and editors.

641
00:58:14,926 --> 00:58:19,492
If your agent and client support ACP, you have a perfect match.

642
00:58:20,238 --> 00:58:29,252
To make this plug and play even easier, the ACP registry automates finding, installing, and connecting agents within clients.

643
00:58:29,646 --> 00:58:34,884
The ACP registry covers the whole life cycle, making it easier to mix and match.

644
00:58:35,918 --> 00:58:43,652
For example, in JetBrains IDEs, the AI chat window leads to installation from the ACP registry.

645
00:58:44,142 --> 00:58:47,812
The IDE can then show a listing of compatible agents.

646
00:58:49,230 --> 00:58:51,748
OpenCode is a popular open source agent.

647
00:58:52,142 --> 00:58:56,292
The ACP registry makes it easy to add to our IDE.

648
00:58:56,910 --> 00:59:05,092
Clicking install automates both the installation of OpenCode itself if needed, which is nice, and integration into the IDE.

649
00:59:06,030 --> 00:59:11,204
Once finished, your IDE now has native integration with a new agent.

650
00:59:12,206 --> 00:59:19,844
You can use this new agent alongside the other agents in your same editor as part of SDD.

651
00:59:21,070 --> 00:59:26,720
These new agent standards are bringing new possibilities, but how do they actually work?

652
00:59:27,278 --> 00:59:32,580
The ACP architecture was designed to ease plugging together agents and clients.

653
00:59:33,070 --> 00:59:36,420
In fact, the protocol matches what's used in LSP.

654
